{
  "title": "Home Buying Checklist: Printable Step-by-Step Guide",
  "metaDescription": "Build a customized, printable home buying checklist matched to your loan, property type, and local hazards. Track every step from pre-approval to closing.",
  "h1": "Home Buying Checklist",
  "introText": "Buying a house involves six stages: budget and credit, mortgage pre-approval, house hunting, offer and contract contingencies, inspection and appraisal, and insurance and closing. Which tasks apply to your purchase depends on your financing, property type, and property location, and the tool above creates that personalized list for you.\n\nTo build your home buying checklist, select your home type, build year, and payment method in the tool. Turn on the local environmental conditions that match your town, or pick your state to apply initial presets. The tool drops items that do not apply to your situation, updates your item count in your browser, and lets you print the customized worksheet using the Print or save as PDF button.",
  "toolHeading": "Build your home buying checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Answer a few questions about the property, your financing, and local climate risks to generate your tailored worksheet.",
  "howItWorks": "The home buying process moves across six core stages. First, you review your budget, credit profile, and savings for upfront expenses. You can project your numbers with the [home affordability calculator](/mortgage/home-affordability-calculator/), the [down payment calculator](/mortgage/down-payment-calculator/), and the [closing cost calculator](/mortgage/closing-cost-calculator/). The second stage covers mortgage pre-approval, followed by the third stage of house hunting. The fourth stage involves structuring your purchase offer and selecting contract contingencies. The fifth stage handles physical evaluations, linking directly to our [home inspection checklist](/checklists/home-inspection-checklist/) and lender appraisals. The sixth stage wraps up homeowners insurance and the final walkthrough before closing.\n\nYour financing choice immediately reshapes your required tasks. When you buy with cash, the entire mortgage pre-approval section hides. If you choose a government-backed loan, specific federal rules apply. Federal Housing Administration (FHA) loans add mortgage insurance items and minimum property requirements. Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) loans require obtaining a Certificate of Eligibility. Both FHA and VA appraisals verify minimum property requirements, meaning any flagged repairs must be completed before closing. For a United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) loan, the address must sit in an eligible area, which you can verify directly on the official [USDA eligibility map](https://eligibility.sc.egov.usda.gov/eligibility/welcomeAction.do).\n\nThe property type and year of construction determine another set of checks. Buying a condo or townhouse adds homeowner association review tasks, including examining HOA dues and documents. Condo purchases using FHA or VA financing also add a condo project approval verification. For homes built before 1978, federal law requires sellers to disclose known lead-based paint and provide the Environmental Protection Agency pamphlet titled Protect Your Family From Lead in Your Home. Under the [EPA real estate disclosure](https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosure) regulations, buyers receive a 10-day window to test for lead hazards unless mutually waived in writing. \n\nLocal environmental hazards introduce location-specific inspections. Setting the switches for freezing winter regions adds checks for pipes running through unheated spaces, heating service, and locating the main water shutoff valve. In coastal hurricane areas, the checklist adds wind mitigation checks, such as roof-to-wall attachments, storm shutters, and verifying separate wind or hurricane deductibles on your insurance quote. Selecting a wildfire zone prompts tasks for defensible space clearance, ember-resistant vents, and insurer availability. For earthquake-prone regions, tasks focus on foundation bolting and water heater strapping, while reminding buyers that standard policies exclude earthquake damage.\n\nLoan timing rules protect your financing schedule. Under Consumer Financial Protection Bureau regulations, your lender must deliver an official [Loan Estimate](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-estimate-en-1995/) within three business days of receiving your completed application. Later in the purchase, your lender must provide the [Closing Disclosure](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-closing-disclosure-en-1983/) at least three business days before you sign final closing documents. Before transferring closing funds, call your escrow or title company at a number you already know to confirm wiring instructions and prevent wire fraud.\n\nYour immediate next step is to answer the questions in the tool above, click Print or save as PDF, and follow the generated tasks stage by stage. While this checklist organizes your milestones, remember it serves as a planning aid rather than legal or financial advice. State laws govern seller disclosure forms and regional closing customs.",
  "workedExample": "In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist's rules for two example homes to evaluate how the list adapts to property and loan profiles. Across the full 59-item library, each scenario kept only the tasks relevant to its specific constraints.\n\nIn Scenario A, an FHA buyer purchasing a condo built after 2000 in Miami, Florida kept 41 of 59 items. That run activated FHA mortgage insurance, FHA condo project approval, FHA minimum property requirement repairs, HOA dues and documents, the roof/wind-resistance check, and the hurricane deductible check, while removing frozen-pipe checks. In Scenario B, a repeat buyer using a conventional mortgage on a 1962 single-family home in Minneapolis kept 37 of 59 items. That run added lead-based paint disclosures, the 10-day lead test period, sewer camera scoping, and freezing-winter plumbing checks, while dropping the condo and hurricane tasks.",
